{
  "step_1": "The decision reason considers whether a lottery has a sure outcome (i.e., an outcome that occurs with 100% probability). Lottery A has three possible outcomes with probabilities 33%, 66%, and 1%, so no outcome in A is certain. Lottery B yields 2400 Euros with 100% probability, so it has a sure outcome. Therefore, the decision reason can be applied: Lottery B has a sure outcome, Lottery A does not. The reason would prefer Lottery B over Lottery A.",
  "step_2": "The individual states: 'Die Wahrscheinlichkeit auf einen hohen Gewinn liegt hier auch bei 99,9% deswegen nehme ich Variante A' (\"The probability of a high gain is also at 99.9%, so I choose option A\"). This suggests the individual perceives a very high chance of receiving a favorable outcome in Lottery A. However, in reality, the highest outcome in A (2500 Euros) occurs only with 33% probability, and the next (2400 Euros) with 66%, totaling 99% chance of a non-zero gain—but not necessarily a 'high' gain. The individual seems to misperceive the probabilities, possibly conflating the 99% chance of winning either 2500 or 2400 with a 99.9% chance of a 'high' gain. The justification centers on the high perceived likelihood of a good outcome in A, despite A lacking a sure outcome. There is no mention of certainty, sure outcomes, or comparing the sureness of results between the lotteries.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring a lottery with a sure outcome, which only Lottery B has. However, the individual chooses A and justifies this based on a (misrepresented) high probability of a 'high gain'—not on the presence or absence of a sure outcome. The individual does not consider whether an outcome is certain; instead, they emphasize near-certainty of a favorable result in A, which is factually inaccurate and conceptually distinct from having a sure outcome. There is no alignment between the information relevant to the decision reason (presence of a sure outcome) and what the individual considered (high probability of a good outcome). Similarly, the justification does not reflect the rule of the decision reason (prefer the lottery with a sure outcome). Therefore, it is certain that the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

